    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find an approximate minimum weighted dominating set.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find an approximate minimum weighted dominating set.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find an approximate minimum weighted dominating set.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind an approximate minimum weighted dominating set.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L78979-L79102){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set(node_weight_attr=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Minimum weighted dominating set](../assets/graphs/minimum-weighted-dominating-set.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The same seven-node graph, with each node's weight w in a badge: the hubs 2 and 5 cost 4, every other node costs 1. The cheapest dominating set found is 1, 4, and 6 (accent), with an accent edge from each other node to the member that dominates it. The card shows [1, 4, 6] and total weight 1 + 1 + 1 = 3.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA dominating set is a set of nodes such that every node in the graph is either in the set\nor adjacent to a member. When nodes have a cost (a weight), the goal is a dominating set\nwith the smallest total cost rather than the fewest nodes. Finding the exact optimum is\nNP-hard, so this method uses a greedy algorithm: at each step it adds the node with the\nlowest weight per newly covered node (the node itself plus its not-yet-covered\nneighbors), and stops once every node is covered. This provides an `O(log n)`\napproximation to the optimal solution.\n\nTypical uses are coverage and placement problems where locations have different costs:\n\n- placing facilities, cell towers, or sensors so that every site is served by one\n  located at it or next door\n- choosing a cheap set of monitors or \"seed\" nodes that touch the whole network\n- selecting a small set of representatives, weighted by how costly each one is to use\n\nWithout `node_weight_attr=`, every node costs `1` and the method becomes the classic greedy\nheuristic for a small (unweighted) dominating set, repeatedly picking the node that\ncovers the most uncovered nodes. Ties are broken in favor of the smaller node ID, so the\nresult is deterministic. The method is intended for undirected graphs: on a directed\ngraph, a chosen node covers only itself and its out-neighbors.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node_weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the node attribute holding each node's cost. If `None` (the default), every\n    node has weight `1`. If given, nodes where the attribute is missing or cannot be\n    converted to a float are assigned weight `1`. Lower weights make a node more\n    attractive to include; use non-negative values.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of node IDs forming an approximate minimum weighted dominating set, sorted in\n    ascending order. An empty graph gives an empty list.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nImplements the Chvátal-style greedy heuristic that at each step picks the node\nminimizing *weight per newly dominated node*: the candidate's weight divided by the\nnumber of uncovered nodes in its closed neighborhood. Each step scans all remaining\ncandidates, so the cost is roughly $O(V \\cdot (V + E))$ in the worst case. The\nresult is a dominating set whose total weight is within an $O(\\log n)$ factor of\noptimal, a bound that matches the best possible polynomial-time approximation ratio\nfor the weighted set-cover problem it generalizes. Nodes with a missing or\nunrecognized `node_weight_attr=` attribute are treated as weight $1$. Note that\n`dominating_set()` uses a different, faster heuristic that does not aim to minimize\nsize, so the two methods can return different sets even when all weights are $1$.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~dominating_set`) \n\n:   A faster sweep with no costs.\n\n[](`~is_dominating_set`) \n\n:   Check a set of nodes.\n\n[](`~minimum_weighted_vertex_cover`) \n\n:   The same kind of problem for edges.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe hub costs more than all three leaves, so the leaves are chosen; without costs, the\nhub alone covers everything:\n\n::: {#8521d7af .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"hub\", \"A\"), (\"hub\", \"B\"), (\"hub\", \"C\")])\ngraph.set_node_attrs(\"cost\", {1: 10, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1})\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n\nweighted = graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set(node_weight_attr=\"cost\")\nplain = graph.minimum_weighted_dominating_set()\n\n[label(n) for n in weighted], [label(n) for n in plain]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(['A', 'B', 'C'], ['hub'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A hall without an access point\n\n`offices()` is the floor plan of a made-up office, with its 20 rooms joined where a door\nleads from one to the other. An access point reaches its own room and the rooms next\ndoor, so the rooms for access points must form a dominating set. If West Hall 1, with\nits glass roof, would cost ten times as much as any other room, the method covers its\nneighbors from three rooms instead, the Lobby, the Kitchen and Meeting A, for six access\npoints in all:\n\n::: {#41582638 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\noffices = ec.offices()\ncost = {\n    n: 10 if offices.get_label_from_id(n) == \"West Hall 1\" else 1\n    for n in offices.get_node_ids()\n}\noffices = offices.set_node_attrs(\"cost\", cost)\npoints = offices.minimum_weighted_dominating_set(node_weight_attr=\"cost\")\n\n[offices.get_label_from_id(n) for n in points]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['Lobby', 'East Hall 1', 'Kitchen', 'Meeting A', 'West Hall 2', 'East Hall 2']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#b15638ed .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\noffices.plot(layout=\"pos\", highlight=points, label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c